@page "/vanilla"

<PageTitle>Vanilla interop</PageTitle>
<h1>Vanilla interop</h1>
<p>Vanilla means Blazor's own (native) form components: this page puts an <code>InputText</code> and a <code>ValidationMessage</code> beside a Formidable input in one form. Both inputs take the same borders, and an entry in the error summary moves focus to either.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "VanillaInterop.razor", "VanillaInterop.razor.cs", "GadgetOrder.cs" })">
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Click <strong>Submit</strong> with both fields empty.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Nickname is required" from Blazor's <code>ValidationMessage</code> and "Colour is required" from Formidable's message component, each under its field, and focus in <strong>Nickname</strong>.</div></li>
            <li>Click the page background, away from both boxes.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> both boxes show the same red border.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> A box with focus wears the blue focus border, whatever its state.</div></li>
            <li>Click "Nickname is required" in the error summary.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> focus moves into <strong>Nickname</strong>, the native input.</div></li>
            <li>Type a nickname into <strong>Nickname</strong> and press Tab.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> its message clears and <strong>Nickname</strong> turns green.</div></li>
            <li>Fill in <strong>Colour</strong> and click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Submitted" in the status line.</div></li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Nickname</strong> (Blazor's <code>InputText</code>): required.</li>
            <li><strong>Colour</strong> (a Formidable input): required.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <HowItWorks>
        <p>A Formidable form installs its own <code>FieldCssClassProvider</code> (Blazor's hook for the CSS classes an input wears). It gives Blazor's own inputs the same classes for invalid, valid and the rest as Formidable's inputs, so both take the same borders with no extra wiring.</p>
        <p>As you edit, the form checks a field once you have changed it, with no submit needed. That holds for <strong>Nickname</strong> as much as for <strong>Colour</strong>.</p>
        <p>A box with focus wears the blue focus border, whatever its state. A blocked submit moves focus to the first failing field on the page, <strong>Nickname</strong> here, so the red borders compare only once neither box has focus.</p>
        <p>The rest of the native input's wiring is written by hand, one concern at a time. A <code>FormidableFieldAnchor</code> (a component that renders nothing) beside the input tells the form the field is on the page, so a submit shows its error.</p>
        <p>Formidable's own inputs carry an element id Formidable derives from the field, and the page gives the native input that same id. A click in the error summary looks for that id and nothing else, so a native input carrying it is as reachable as a Formidable one. <strong>Nickname</strong>'s entry lands in it just as <strong>Colour</strong>'s lands in the Formidable input.</p>
        <p>Three aria attributes give a screen reader what a Formidable input gives it: <code>aria-describedby</code> (the message), <code>aria-invalid</code> (the error state) and <code>aria-required</code>. The page reads each from the form's <code>Engine</code> property (its validation state) and re-renders whenever that state changes, so the first two appear and clear with the message beside the input.</p>
        <p><code>aria-describedby</code> points at the message under the input, but only while there is one. Blazor's <code>ValidationMessage</code> renders nothing at all when there is no message, and an id naming an element that is not there describes nothing.</p>
        <p><code>aria-invalid</code> marks the field as in error. Blazor's own <code>InputText</code> writes it from the field's messages, and this page writes it as well, to show the wiring a control without its own <code>aria-invalid</code> would need. Both answers are <code>true</code> or nothing, so they never disagree.</p>
        <p><code>aria-required</code> says whether the rules a submit checks require the field. It stands whatever the field holds, because whether a field is required belongs to the rules, not to a message.</p>
    </HowItWorks>
    <Demo>
        <FormidableForm Model="_order" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid" @ref="_form">
            <div class="summary-slot">
                <FormidableSummary />
            </div>

            <div class="field">
                <label>Nickname (native InputText) <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _order.Nickname" />
                    <InputText @bind-Value="_order.Nickname"
                               id="@NicknameId"
                               aria-invalid="@NicknameAriaInvalid"
                               aria-describedby="@NicknameAriaDescribedBy"
                               aria-required="@NicknameAriaRequired" /></label>
                <ValidationMessage For="() => _order.Nickname" id="@NicknameMessagesId" />
                <FormidableFieldAnchor For="() => _order.Nickname" />
            </div>

            <div class="field">
                <label>Colour (Formidable input) <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _order.Colour" />
                    <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_order.Colour" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _order.Colour" />
            </div>

            <div class="actions">
                <button type="submit">Submit</button>
            </div>
        </FormidableForm>

        <p role="status">@_status</p>
    </Demo>
</TeachingPanel>
